WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.774
ALEX: Like the difference between
texting your smartest friend versus a
hotline that

00:00:02.774 --> 00:00:05.548
ALEX: picks up immediately.

00:00:05.613 --> 00:00:08.311
JAMIE: Except the hotline is also pretty
smart.

00:00:08.376 --> 00:00:10.334
ALEX: Right, the hotline went to a good
school.

00:00:10.399 --> 00:00:12.617
JAMIE: Heh. What about the context
window?

00:00:12.702 --> 00:00:16.925
ALEX: A million tokens. Which is roughly
a whole novel's worth of text

00:00:16.925 --> 00:00:21.147
ALEX: the model can hold at once — at
the lower price point.

00:00:21.257 --> 00:00:23.724
RUFUS: That's the genuinely useful part
for power users. You can feed it

00:00:23.724 --> 00:00:26.191
RUFUS: a massive codebase and have it
act on the whole thing, not

00:00:26.191 --> 00:00:28.658
RUFUS: just a chunk.

00:00:28.768 --> 00:00:31.645
JAMIE: I'll give it that. But I still
want to know whether "fast"

00:00:31.645 --> 00:00:34.522
JAMIE: means anything if the reasoning
quality drops.
